{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t02-005",
  "track": "track-02",
  "title": "ASP.NET Core MVC controllers actions filters conventions and results",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "MVC controller discovery turns eligible public methods into actions, while attributes and application conventions alter discovery, routing metadata, filters, and API behavior.",
    "An action result is a description of the HTTP response. IActionResult allows different result types; ActionResult<T> combines a typed success value with action results and is useful for API contracts.",
    "Filters run inside MVC's action-invocation pipeline after endpoint selection. Authorization, resource, action, exception, and result filters surround different stages; middleware remains the broader request-pipeline mechanism.",
    "Filter scope, order, and asynchronous/synchronous interfaces determine execution. Dependency-aware filter activation uses service filters, type filters, or factories; careless filter use can duplicate cross-cutting behavior already handled by middleware.",
    "Controller conventions and application models can change behavior across many controllers. Use them deliberately, document implicit rules, and test the resulting endpoint metadata and response behavior."
  ],
